WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.358
Publishing an object means putting a reference where another thread can find it.

2
00:00:07.358 --> 00:00:11.375
Here a loader builds a rate card, and publishes it in a shared field.

3
00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:14.361
That's three steps.

4
00:00:14.361 --> 00:00:19.734
The JVM allocates the object, every field at its default: 0, false or null.

5
00:00:19.734 --> 00:00:22.170
The constructor writes the fields.

6
00:00:22.170 --> 00:00:24.248
Then the reference is stored.

7
00:00:24.467 --> 00:00:28.167
But a reader of a plain field has no edge to these writes.

8
00:00:28.167 --> 00:00:33.973
The JIT may store the reference first, and an ARM CPU may make stores visible in any order.

9
00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:37.882
So the reader finds a card, and still reads a null currency.

10
00:00:37.882 --> 00:00:42.363
That's a partially constructed object, and no line you wrote creates one.

11
00:00:43.267 --> 00:00:44.625
It's not theory.

12
00:00:44.625 --> 00:00:49.551
A jcstress sample does exactly this, with four int fields.

13
00:00:49.551 --> 00:00:55.326
On x86_64, readers saw zeros in about 18,000 of 219 million samples.

14
00:00:55.326 --> 00:00:59.912
On AArch64, almost every mix of zeros and real values.

15
00:01:00.133 --> 00:01:02.615
x86 keeps its stores in order.

16
00:01:02.615 --> 00:01:05.757
So on x86, the JIT did the reordering.

17
00:01:06.567 --> 00:01:07.485
Two ways out.

18
00:01:07.485 --> 00:01:22.324
First, publish through a happens-before edge, so the reference and the state travel together: a static initializer, a volatile field or AtomicReference, a final field, a lock, or a java.util.concurrent handoff.

19
00:01:23.633 --> 00:01:27.226
Second, make the object safe to see even through a race.

20
00:01:27.226 --> 00:01:29.023
That's what final fields do.

21
00:01:29.233 --> 00:01:35.409
When a constructor writes a final field, HotSpot's C2 compiler puts a store-store barrier at its end.

22
00:01:35.409 --> 00:01:41.279
Every write before it completes before any store after it, and the publishing store comes after.

23
00:01:41.533 --> 00:01:46.286
On x86 the barrier compiles to nothing: it only stops the JIT.

24
00:01:46.286 --> 00:01:48.203
On ARM it's a real fence.

25
00:01:48.433 --> 00:01:57.339
So the rule says: a thread that sees the reference only after the constructor finished sees the final fields as the constructor set them.

26
00:01:57.339 --> 00:01:58.964
Even through a data race.

27
00:01:59.167 --> 00:02:05.744
In the jcstress sample, four final fields removed every partial result, on both machines.

28
00:02:06.667 --> 00:02:09.819
But final fields don't make the reference visible.

29
00:02:09.819 --> 00:02:13.475
A reader spinning on a plain field may never see the card.

30
00:02:13.475 --> 00:02:16.248
So do both: final fields and a safe publish.

31
00:02:16.248 --> 00:02:18.896
And don't let this escape the constructor.
